2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/0e/af/7a/b1/5344485ed8bbbbd2/netphen_st_martins_church_back_view_original.jpg?hash=4c147b7023ac97ee47cc468ef99760e4ed7811e71b16aa3aa52d0b2d75f5f88c.1795161599
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL St. Martin's Church, Netphen
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE The Rev. Henry Haeger was baptized in the Martini Church in Netphen, this church dating back to 1239 and was one of the earliest parishes in the Siegerland.  The church in Netphen was of the Reformed faith, the church available to
3 CONC  the Reformed people that lived in the Catholic district of which Anzhausen was a part. The Reformed people were persecuted because of their faith, their churches taken away, their ministers and teachers were driven out and their 
3 CONC children were forced to attend the Catholic schools.  This persecution took place under the Catholic Prince, William Hyacinth, even resulting in the public execution of Friedrich Flender in 1707 on the grounds of the Upper Castle 
3 CONC in Siegen.
3 CONT
3 CONT Johann Henrich Haeger was born in Anzhausen on 25 Sept 1644 and baptized at the Martinikirche, the son of Henrich Haeger and the widow, Guda Schram, of Wilgersdorf.  Henrich Haeger was the schoolmaster in Anzhausen and the family 
3 CONC was living there until at least 1662.
3 CONT
3 CONT The information was taken from the following sources and I took the photo of the Church on the 2013 Germanna Foundation Germany Trip--Barb Price
3 CONT
3 CONT William J. Hinke, The First German Reformed Colony in Virginia: 1714-1750, (The Journal of the Presbyterian Historical Society (1901-1930), Vol. 2, No. 1 June 1903). 1-17. 
3 CONT B. C. Holtzclaw, Germanna Record No. 5 Ancestry and Descendants of the Nassau Siegen Immigrants 1714-1750, [Culpeper, VA: The Memorial Foundation of the Germanna Colonies in Virginia, Inc. 1964] 20.
3 CONT Ibid, Hinke. 7.
3 CONT Ibid, Holtzclaw. 170.
