2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/25130478/jacob-hager
2 CONT
2 CONT Inscription "In Memory Of Capt. Jacob Hager who departed this life Aug 21, 1819 in the 85 year of his age"
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. Jacob Hager was the son of Johann Heinrich and Anna Gertrude (Petrie) Hager. He was the husband of Cornelia (Vrooman) Hager. They were married July 8, 1764. Cornelia died May 22, 1826. Jacob served during the Revolutionary W
2 CONC ar and the French and Indian War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary The Albany Gazette September 6, 1819
2 CONT
2 CONT Roscoe, William E. History of Schoharie County, New York Blenheim Chapter XVI
2 CONT
2 CONT Town of Blenheim Monument - Honor Roll Capt. Jacob Hager Schoharie County (NY) Militia - October 20, 1775 Served in Schoharie and Mohawk Valleys Throughout the Revolutionary War Commanded Expedition Against Brant Defended Upper Fo
2 CONC rt Against Sir John Johnson in 1780 Died May 21, 1819 - Aged 85 years Erected by his descendants and the State of New York - 1928
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Hager spent his boyhood at the German Flats in the Mohawk Valley of New York. His father, Henry, purchased 78.5 acres of land in the Breakabean area, and he and his mother & four brothers moved there. During the French and I
2 CONC ndian War he served as a lieutenant in the British army under Capt. Sternberg. In 1775 he was appointed captain in the Second Company, 15th Regiment of the New York Militia. He was in command of the Upper Fort on Schoharie during 
2 CONC the Revolutionary War. He is mentioned in mentioned in "Revolutionary Rangers" by Richard B. LaCrosse, Jr. and in "The History of Schoharie County and the Border Wars of New York" by Jeptha Simms. Read about Jacob in "The Destruct
2 CONC ion of Vroomans Land" album. Jacob`s obituary was printed in the Albany "Ploughboy" dated 28 August, 1819 and reads as follows: August 28, 1819 Died at Blenheim, Schoharie county, on Saturday the 21st inst. of a lingering disease,
2 CONC  Capt.. Jacob Hager, in the 85th year of his age. It may be truly said, that he was a true friend to his country, a patron and a soldier. He served in the French war, and as a captain through the revolution of 1776, on the frontie
2 CONC rs, where he distinguished himself, and risked his life in many instances, to gain our independence. He was a faithful friend, an affectionate husband, and a kind father. He descended to the grave, like a ripe shock of corn, and d
2 CONC ied in the full faith of his Redeemer.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Hagar-218
2 CONT
