2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Rector-116 WikiTree Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 15 2021, 18:15:59 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John Johannes Rector was son of Hans Jacob Richter (apparently after coming from Germany, most of the descendents spelled their last name: Rector). He was born in Trupbach, Westfalen, Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/13039353/john-jacob-rector Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 15 2021, 18:17:33 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Per Alix V., email of 05-08-1999:  "Buried Rectortown Cemetery Rectortown, Fauquier Co., VA.  Will is in B.Il, 205-207, Fauquier Co. Records, dated 5 Nov. 1772, probated 22 Mar 1773.  Cited in Willis Miller Kemper, Genealogy of th
2 CONC e Fischback Family in America, 1914. p. 83 Library of Congress Call Number CS71.F5325 1914.  ."  Also: "See also Josiah Shinn, Pioneers and Makers of Arkansas; 3 Arkansas governors are his descendants."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Immigrated to Germanna, Va. in 1714 with his parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/13039353/john-jacob-rector
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1772 John Rector, a German immigrant, came to Virginia, and settled on 4,872 acres of land on what is now Route 713.  Rectortown, named after its founder, became a busy agricultural center ... soon the village incorporated,  an
2 CONC d Rectortown became Fauquier county's first town.  [http://www.mtoliverectortown.org/pdf/History.pdf History of the Mount Olive Baptist Church]
2 CONT
2 CONT >Rectortown, Virginia, originally called Maidstone in honor of Lord Fairfax's family seat in England, was founded in 1772 and is thought to be Fauquier's oldest town. It has a history as colorful as it is long.
2 CONT
2 CONT >In 1786, for example, George Washington gave a tart assessment of the village in a letter to a nephew who had foolishly, to the Founding Father's mind, bought land there. Maidstone, he wrote, "originated with and will end in two 
2 CONC or three gin shops, which will exist no longer than they serve to ruin the proprietors and those who make the most frequent applications to them." [http://articles.washingtonpost.com/2008-11-15/news/36773299_1_maidstone-village-po
2 CONC st-office-houses A Sense of History, but No Old Gin Shops] Washington Post, 2008
2 CONT
2 CONT John's son Benjamin was found in the will of another son of John, Henry .
2 CONT
2 CONT John's will was written 5 Jul 1772 (it is available in the Fauquier Cou rthouse in Virginia). In it he mentions his wife, Catherine (to whom h e leaves his plantation); sons Henry, Daniel, Charles, Jacob, Benjamin, a nd Frederick;
2 CONC  daughters Catherine children (she was deceased at the tim e of the will) and Elizabeth. He does not mention his son John III, wh o had evidently died also, but he does mention John III's son John, and a nd his (John II's) brother
2 CONC  Harmon. It was probated 22 Mar 1773. Test H enry Rector and Jacob Faubion.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his will, he left:
2 CONT
2 CONT *     His wife: The plantation "I live on", 300 acres during her life then to s on Henry;
2 CONT *     Son Henry: 224 acres plus the 300 acres after wife's death;
2 CONT *     Son Daniel: A bequest, but no land;
2 CONT *     Son Charles: 201 acres called the Race Ground Lott;
2 CONT *     Son Jacob: Several negroes, etc.;
2 CONT *     Son Benjamin: The plantation on which he (Benjamin) lives, 106 acres;
2 CONT *     Son Frederick: A bequest, but no land;
2 CONT *     Grandson John (son of John III): 100 acres;
2 CONT *     Grandson Harmon (son of John III): 100 acres, on which he (Harmon) liv es on Licking Run.
2 CONT *     After wife's death, the plantation property (tools, instruments, etc.) t o be divided into eight equal parts and divided among sons John, Daniel , Jacob, Charles, Benjamin, and Frederick, and "the children of my daug hter El
2 CONC izabeth, now living".
2 CONT *     Wife and son, Henry, were executors; test. Henry Rector, Jacob Faubion, a nd John Adams
2 CONT *     On 17 Dec 1770, Mary and Burgess Ball, and John Rector, planter, bought 1 900 acres of land near Goose Creek; one of the witnesses was John's son , Henry. John (the 2nd) was very active in buying and selling land for p rofit
2 CONC . He sold his old land near Germantown before moving to the north ern part of the Fauquier County.
2 CONT *     John (II) was living in Fauquier Co., VA, in 1958-Source: List of tith ables.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==============================================
2 CONT
2 CONT Extract of Will:
2 CONT
2 CONT *"IN THE NAME OF GOD AMEN: the fifth day of July, in the year of our Lo rdChrist one thousand seven hundred and seventy two, I, John Rector of t he County of Fauquier and Colony of Virginia, being very sick and week o f Body but o
2 CONC f sound and disposing memory, thanks be to Almighty God for t he same, and, calling to mind the brevity and uncertainty of this morta l Life, knowing that it is appointed for all men once to die, do theref ore make and ordain this
2 CONC  my last will and Testament; that is to say, fi rst and principally: I Give and recommend my Precious and immortal sou l into the hands of Almighty God who gave it to me, and my body I commi t to the Earth to be buried in a decent
2 CONC  Christian like manner, at the d iscretion of my Executor, hereinafter named; and, as Touching such worl dly Goods and Estate as it hath pleased almighty God to Bless me withal i n this life, I Give, Devise, and Dispose of the sam
2 CONC e, in manner and for m following VIZ:
2 CONT * Item, I give and bequeath to my well beloved wife Catherine the plantat ion whereon I now Dwell containing three hundred acres of Land, be the s ame more or less, with all the profits, Benefits and advantages, thereu nto Belongi
2 CONC ng or in any wise appertaining together with all my negroes, l ikewise all my household and Kitchen Furniture and plantation Utensils, a lso all my stock of every kind and Denomination to be enjoyed by her pe aceable and Quietly w
2 CONC ithout lett, molestation or disturbance for and du ring her natural life, and, after her decease, I Give and dispose of th e same in the following manner. VIZ:
2 CONT *    Item, I give and bequeath to my son Henry all that part of parcel of La nd whereupon Joseph Nell now dwells, containing two hundred and twenty f our acres, be the same more or less, likewise my now dwelling plantatio n contai
2 CONC ning three hundred acres of Land, be the same more or less, to b e enjoyed by him and his heirs, forever after the Decease of my wife, C atherine, at which time he shall pay the full sum of Fifty pounds curre nt money of Virginia,
2 CONC  to my son Daniel or his heirs.
2 CONT *    Item, I give and bequeath to my son Charles all that part or parcel of L and adjoining his plantation commonly known by the name of the Racegrou nd, Lot containing two hundred and one Acres, be the same more or less, t o be e
2 CONC njoyed by him and his Heirs forever. I likewise Give to my said s on Charles one negro woman called Cate and one negro boy called James, t o him and his Heirs after the decease of my wife Catherine.
2 CONT *    Item, I Give and bequeath to my son Jacob one negro man called Anthony a nd likewise my Clock after the decease of my wife Catherine; also I giv e to my said son Jacob my smith's bellows and anvil and tools of all ki nd there
2 CONC unto, belonging to him and his Heirs.
2 CONT *    Item, I Give and bequeat to my son Benjamin the plantation whereon he n ow Dwells, containing one hundred and Sixty Acres of Land, more or less , to be enjoyed by him and his Heirs forever; also, I Give and bequeath t o my so
2 CONC n Frederick one negro boy called Jeffey and one negro boy called J ackey, to him and his Heirs, after the decease of my wife Catherine.
2 CONT *    Item, I Give and bequeath to my Granson John Rector, son of my son John , one hundred Acres of Land Adjoining my son Frederick's Plantation of C romwell's Run, to be enjoyed by him and his Heirs forever.
2 CONT *    Item, I Give to my Brother Harmon Rector one hundred acres of Land wher eon he now Dwells, Lying on Licking Run, to him and his Heirs forever.
2 CONT *    Item, I will, and it is my desire, that after the decease of my well be loved wife Catherine afs'd, then all my household and kitchen Furniture l ikewise, my stock of all kinds and denominations, also my two details a nd all 
2 CONC my plantation Utensils be exposed to public Sale and the amount t hereof to be divided into Eight equal parts; one Eighth part thereof I G ive to my son John, one Eighth thereof to my son Daniel, one Eighth the reof to my son Jaco
2 CONC b, one Eighth thereof to my son Charles, one Eighth t hereof to my son Benjamin, one Eighth thereof to my son Frederick, one E ighth thereof to be equally divided among the children of my daughter C atherine, now living, and I do 
2 CONC hereby constitute and appoint my well B eloved wife Catherine and my son Henry to be joint Executrix and Execut or of this my Last will and Testament, hereby revoking and disannulling a ll Wills by me heretofore made, Ratifying an
2 CONC d confirming this and none o ther to be my last will and Testament; in Testimony whereof I have here unto set my hand and affixed my seal the day and year above Written.
2 CONT *    Signed, Sealed and Delivered. L.S.
2 CONT *    His John x Rector Mark
2 CONT *    In the Presence of us
2 CONT *    His Henry X Rector Mark
2 CONT *    His Jacob x Faubion Mark
2 CONT *    John Adams.
2 CONT *    "At a Court held for Farquier County the 22nd da of March 1773, This wi ll was proved by the oaths of Henry Rector and John Adams, Witnesses th ereto, and ordered to be recorded, and, on the motion of Catherine Rect or and He
2 CONC nry Rector, the Executors therein named, who made oath and exe cuted and Acknowledge bond as the Law directs, Certificate is granted t hem for obtaining a probate thereof in due form.
2 CONT *    Teste: H. Brooke Clerk
2 CONT *    A Copy Teste: J.E. Bartenstein.
2 CONT *    Clerk of Circuit Court, Fauquier Co., Va. Will Book 1 page 205."
2 CONT
