2 CONT Fauquier Co Virginia pg 87-88 John Glascock will, 8 March 1765''' wife Mary''' half of estate, real and personal dau Frances other half JOHN GLASCOCK exors Mary Glascock and Thomas Glascock wit: Gregory Glascock, Aaron Drummond, J
2 CONC ohn Hendren proved 22 April 1765, by oath of Aaron Drummond and John Hendren Thomas Glascock gave bond and was granted cert of probate
2 CONT
2 CONT Inventory court order dated 27 May 1765 approved by Simon Miller, John Obanon, William Obannon returned 24 June 1765
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Jospeh Goddard enlisted at the age of 16. He married Oct 10, 1779 to  Frances Glasscock the step daughter of Henry Rector. He later moved to  Fleming Co, Kentucky with Burtis Ringo whose wife Hannah Rector, was a  cousin of France
2 CONC s. Ringo also served in the 10th regiment. Frances mother was Mary Henren who married 1st. November 28, 1759 to John  Glasscock. Joseph was 24 years old, married with two children when they settled in  Ohio County, Va (now WV). La
2 CONC ter they moved on to Kentucky.  His father John relocated his family from Fauquier County Va, in about  1785. They first aimed to settle in the Northwest Territory in what is  now Harrison Co. Ohio. Ejected from there as squatters
2 CONC  by the U. S. Army,  they settled or were resettled in Ohio and Brooks counties of VA (WV).  They lived there about 10 years, until 1795, when John Sr. and Mary moved  to Fleming County, Kentucky.  Joseph and John seem to have own
2 CONC ed land ajacent to each other in many  locations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia Northern Neck Land Grants, 1742-1775. [Vol. II] Record for Virginia Northern Neck Land Grants, 1742-1775. [Vol. II]  M-200 JOSHUA GORE JR of Loudoun Co 700 acres on North side Blew Ridge in Frederick Co suv Mr. John Hough
2 CONC  adj Edward Snickars, George William Fairfax Esq, Col Mann Page, foot of Piney Mt, Right Hon Thomas Lord Fairfax 10 Oct 1763
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "History of Northeastern Nelson County" by Robert P. Moore:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gore, This Loudoun Co., Va. family is the more numerous of the two families by that name in Nelson Co., both of which lived in or near Bloomfield. The first member of this family who can be identified with certainty is Joshua Gore
2 CONC , died 1785, Loudoun Co., Va. (Will Book C, p. 150). His first marriage was to probably to Elizabeth Clowes or Clews of a Quaker family. Her father was Thomas Clowes/Clews/Close, born ca. 1707, died 7 Aug. 1784, Loudoun Co., Va. (
2 CONC Will Book C, p. 84), married 4 Oct. 1728, Falls Meeting, Bucks Co., Pa., Christian Palmer, daughter of John Palmer and  Christian 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joshua Gore married (2) Mrs. Mary (Glasscock) Rector, born 30 July 1734, died before 1820 (will was written 28 Sept. l813), Fauquier Co., Va., daughter of John Glasscock of Fauquier Co., Va., died ca. 1784, and Margaret O'Rear. Ma
2 CONC ry was the widow of another John Glasscock and of Harry Rector [Richter]. Joshua Gore was on the Loudoun Co. tithable list of 1765. That Hannah was probably their oldest child is indicated in a three-lives lease from John Tayloe o
2 CONC f Richmond Co., Va. to Joshua, Elizabeth, and Hannah on 10 Aug. 1758 (Loudoun Co. Deed Book A, p. 229). There.is a similar deed between these two parties in Loudoun Deed Book E, p. 138. Joshua's son Jonathan married  his step-sist
2 CONC er Margaret Rector, and another son, Henry married Ann Glasscock, daughter of Micajah Glasscock and Catherine Rector. Their sister Charlotte married into the Marshall family of Fairfield, noted as furniture makers. The Gores also 
2 CONC had ties with the northeasten Nelson Co. families of McKay, Duncan and Bodine. There is a Gore cemetery in the country outside Fairfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonathan Gore, b. 7 June 1766, Loudoun Co., Va., d. 1 Sept 1849, Nelson Co., Ky. (will written 22 May 1848, prob. 10 Sept. 1849), son of Joshua Gore, m. (l) Margaret Rector, b. 10 March 1770, probably Fauquier Co., Va., d. 7 Jan. 
2 CONC 1833, Nelson Co., Ky., (2) Rebecca Leach, b. 5 Jan. 1783, d. 10 March 1856. Margaret Rector Gore was the daughter of Jonathan's stepmother Mary Glasscock, by Henry ("Harry") Rector. A good deal of research has been done and publis
2 CONC hed on both the Glasscock and Rector families. Henry Rector Was the son of John Rector and Anna Catherine Fishback. John Rector's will, written 5 Nov. 1772, proved 22 March 1773, is in Fauquier Co. Will Book 1, pp. 205-7.  Margare
2 CONC t Rector Gore had a sister Catherine Rector who m. Micajah Glasscock of Nelson Co., Ky. Jonathan and both his wives are buried in the Gore-Glasscock cem, near Fairfield, Nelson Co., Ky. (In 1992 this graveyard was on the farm of H
2 CONC arold Moran, who has reset the stones.) It is located about 3.5 miles from Fairfield on Murray's Run Road.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Fauquier Co Virginia pg 87-88 John Glascock will, 8 March 1765 wife Mary half of estate, real and personal dau Frances other half JOHN GLASCOCK exors Mary Glascock and Thomas Glascock wit: Gregory Glascock, Aaron Drummond, John He
2 CONC ndren proved 22 April 1765, by oath of Aaron Drummond and John Hendren Thomas Glascock gave bond and was granted cert of probate
2 CONT
2 CONT Inventory court order dated 27 May 1765 approved by Simon Miller, John Obanon, William Obannon returned 24 June 1765
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Joshua Gorr, of Loudoun Co., Va.
2 CONT
2 CONT ... to my son, Thomas Gore, five shillings, he being already provided for... ... to William Osborne my negro girl, Winney… to my daughter, Betsey, my negro girl, Hannah... to my wife a bay mare which I got from Timothy Howell last
2 CONC  Fall and two cows... to my wife the sheep which she brought on this plantation... to my wife all the goods and effects whatsoever I became possessed of in consequence of my marriage with her and effects which she became possessed
2 CONC  of by the death of her former husband, Harry Rector... excepting such articles as I have paid for to the Ex'ors of Harry Rector's Estate... I give and bequeath to my sons, Joshua and Jonathan Gore, to be equally divided between t
2 CONC hem, the following negros: Jude, Harry, Janney, Jam, Will, and Warner... my two sons Josua and Jonathan to be my Executors... to sell the plantation whereon I now live...
2 CONT
2 CONT Wit. Abner Osborn, James Kitchen, Isaac Brown, William Neilson
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed, his Josua Gorr mark
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Jospeh Goddard enlisted at the age of 16. He married Oct 10, 1779 to  Frances Glasscock the step daughter of Henry Rector. He later moved to  Fleming Co, Kentucky with Burtis Ringo whose wife Hannah Rector, was a  cousin of France
2 CONC s. Ringo also served in the 10th regiment. Frances mother was Mary Henren who married 1st. November 28, 1759 to John  Glasscock. Joseph was 24 years old, married with two children when they settled in  Ohio County, Va (now WV). La
2 CONC ter they moved on to Kentucky.  His father John relocated his family from Fauquier County Va, in about  1785. They first aimed to settle in the Northwest Territory in what is  now Harrison Co. Ohio. Ejected from there as squatters
2 CONC  by the U. S. Army,  they settled or were resettled in Ohio and Brooks counties of VA (WV).  They lived there about 10 years, until 1795, when John Sr. and Mary moved  to Fleming County, Kentucky.  Joseph and John seem to have own
2 CONC ed land ajacent to each other in many  locations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia Northern Neck Land Grants, 1742-1775. [Vol. II] Record for Virginia Northern Neck Land Grants, 1742-1775. [Vol. II]  M-200 JOSHUA GORE JR of Loudoun Co 700 acres on North side Blew Ridge in Frederick Co suv Mr. John Hough
2 CONC  adj Edward Snickars, George William Fairfax Esq, Col Mann Page, foot of Piney Mt, Right Hon Thomas Lord Fairfax 10 Oct 1763
2 CONT
2 CONT
