2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Atwood-726
2 CONT
2 CONT The followng information comes from Harold Boles and David Boles, Foster Ancestors: Some Europeans, Immigrants, Colonists, and Pioneers (1990, privately published), p. 127, which is thoroughly documented -- see image attached to t
2 CONC his profile.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Atwood was born presumably around 1749; he lived and died in Culpeper County. He married Catharine Fishback around 1771. Catharine appears as "Catharine Atwood" in the 1782 will of her father, Frederick Fishback.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 5 November 1771, James Atwood and William Atwood appear on a Culpeper County list of people who bought items from the estate of Courtney Norman.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 Sept. 1782, James's father-in-law Frederick Fishback wrote his will, naming daughter Catharine Atwood among his children.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 Aug. 1783 Jacob Fishback and wife Phebe deeded to James Atwood, for £250, a tract of land "in the Little Fork of Rappahannock River" cornering land owned by John Kemper. Witnesses were Thomas Spelman, Oliver Clark, and Lewis
2 CONC  Corbin.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 10 Sept. 1784, James and Catharine Atwood participated in the sale of 206 acres jointly inherited from Catherine's brother Frederick (Jr.), who had died childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 21 March 1785 James Atwood received, for five shillings and "for the natural love and affection which they bear unto the said James Atwood," from Martin and Lucy Fishback and Harman and Sarah Button, twenty acres of land "in th
2 CONC e Little Fork of Rappahannock River" in Culpeper County.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Atwood appears in the 1787 Culpeper County personal property tax list with zero white tithables (except for himself) 3 blacks aged 16 and over; 2 blacks under 16, 3 horses, and 10 cattle.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Atwood died soon after the birth of his daughter Elizabeth in 1799. His widow "Caty" appears in the Culpeper County personal tax list in 1800, with 2 white males 16+, 2 slaves 16+, and 2 slaves 12-16.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 13 Aug. 1802, James Atwood's four eldest (adult) children, with three spouses, sold 142 acres of land in Culpeper County to Samuel Wood for £200; this was apparently part of the settlement of his estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT * Harold W. Boles and David B. Boles, Foster Ancestors: some Europeans, immigrants, colonists and pioneers (Kalamazoo, Mich., 1990), p. 127. [https://www.wikitree.com/photo.php/2/27/Atwood-726.jpg Link] 
