0 @N6000000006348278002@ NOTE James Kemper was the great uncle of General James Lawson Kemper, noted Confederate general in Pickett's division.
1 CONT
1 CONT   53                  JAMES KEMPER. 
1 CONT
1 CONT                 B. "Cedar Grove," Va., 23 Nov., 175
1 CONT                 D. "Walnut Hills," Cincinnati, O., 20 Aug
1 CONT                     1834, of cholera. 
1 CONT                 M. In Fauquier Co., Va., 16 July, 177
1 CONT                     JUDITH HATHAWAY. 
1 CONT                 B. Near "White Stone," Lancaster Co., Va
1 CONT                      26 Apr., 1756. 
1 CONT                 D. "Walnut Hills," O., 1 Mch., 1846. 
1 CONT
1 CONT She was a dau. of John and Sarah (Timberlake) Hathaway, and gr. dau. of William and Sarah (Lawson) Hathaway, and of Frances and Judith (Lawson) Timberlake, of Lancaster and Northumberland Counties, Va. James and Judith Kemper are 
1 CONC both buried at "Spring Grove" cemetery, Cincinnati, Hamilton Co., OH.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Issue: 
1 CONT       154    I. Frances.?? 
1 CONT       155   II. Elnathan.?? 
1 CONT       156  III. Sarah Lawson.?? 
1 CONT       157   IV. Caleb.?? 
1 CONT       158    V. Peter Hathaway.?? 
1 CONT       159   VI. Edward Young.?? 
1 CONT       160  VII. James, Jr.?? 
1 CONT       161 VIII. John Flavel, b. Garrard Co., Ky., 1 Mch., 178
1 CONT                       Killed, Powhatan Co., Va., by Northe
1 CONT                       Soldiers, 1864, n. m. 
1 CONT       162   IX. Judith Blair.?? 
1 CONT       163    X. Samuel Dodridge.?? 
1 CONT       164   XI. David Rice.?? 
1 CONT       165  XII. Sophia Elizabeth.?? 
1 CONT       166 XIII. Agnes Susan Timberlake.?? 
1 CONT       167  XIV. Frederick Augustus.?? 
1 CONT       168   XV. Charles Adolphus Benjamin.?? 
1 CONT
1 CONT JAMES KEMPER, after his marriage in 1772, settled on a farm near his father's "Cedar Grove," place, where he lived until 1783. His health had been impaired by over work and he taught school for about a year. Partly through the per
1 CONC suasion of a friend, Rev. Hezekiah Balch, and also of his cousin, Jacob Fishback, and partly with the expectation of bettering his fortunes, Mr. Kemper and his family, then consisting of his wife and six children, moved in 1783 to
1 CONC  Western North Carolina, south of the Cumberland Mountains, on Richland Creek. The locality was known as Nolachucky. He had procured a commission as Deputy Surveyor of a large and valuable tract of country, which made it easy for 
1 CONC him to secure lands of the best quality. His friend and his cousin had previously located there, but soon after James Kemper arrived the latter removed to Mercer Co., Ky. Mr. Kemper had for a long time been deeply interested in re
1 CONC ligious matters and had a desire to fit himself for the ministry. His cousin arranged to have him come to Ky., to study with Rev. David Rice, D. D., and accordingly he made the removal in April, 1785. Here he had a hard struggle t
1 CONC o support himself and family, but managed to accomplish his purpose of preparing himself for the ministry, through the kind assistance of Dr. Rice, and of his cousin. In addition to his farm work and his studies, he taught school 
1 CONC and acted as public chatechist, the first one west of the Allegheny Mountains. He was licensed to preach by the Presbytery of Transylvania, on 7 Oct., 1790. In Dec. of that year, through the friendly offices of Dr. Rice, he first 
1 CONC visited Cincinnati, where there was no Presbyterian minister. As a result of this visit and of a second one made in the following spring, he removed his family to Cincinnati, reaching there Oct. 25, 1791; just nine days before the
1 CONC  defeat of Gen'l St. Clair by the Indians. During the first year his congregation raised a good frame house of worship 30 x 40 feet, two stories high, which became in due course of time the First Presbyterian Church of Cincinnati.
1 CONC  The congregation used to bring their fire arms to the services on account of the Indians. The Indian war was raging and the church was reduced to one member. In 1792 he was regularly called as pastor for a term of three years; Pr
1 CONC esbytery attended at Cincinnati, in October, 1792, under a guard, and ordained Rev. James Kemper, pastor. Soon after this he purchased 130 acres of land about two miles from his church, on Walnut Hills, and in 1793 removed there, 
1 CONC to a high block house, which he occupied until 1804, when a more commodious house was built, which is still standing and in good preservation. Almost simultaneously with the close of his three years pastorate the Indian war came t
1 CONC o an end and a large part of his congregation scattered to their farms. From 1795 to 1810 he preached regularly, but in widely scattered settlements between the Little Miami and the Great Miami Rivers, and made many missionary tou
1 CONC rs, through Kentucky and Virginia, by the appointment of the General Assembly. In 1810 he received a call to the church at Flemingsburg, Ky., which he accepted, and moved there in May, 1810. He remained there four years and left b
1 CONC ecause the congregation would not build what he considered a suitable house of worship--but he says of this later: "In this I erred capitally." He returned to his former home on Walnut Hills and resumed occasional preaching servic
1 CONC es in the country around there, until the formation of a church on Walnut Hills. He and his sons and neighbors built a stone church on the hill in 1818, and there Mr. Kemper performed his ministerial duties uninterruptedly until h
1 CONC is death. In a historical paper prepared for the Cincinnati Presbytery, by Rev. Joseph G. Monfort, D. D., he says: "Perhaps no man in the valley of the Mississippi has been a first pioneer in so many places and departments as Jame
1 CONC s Kemper. He was the first Catechist ever appointed west of the Alleghenies and south of Virginia; the first student of theology; the first licentiate of the First Presbytery, the first supply north of the Ohio, in answer to the f
1 CONC irst request for preaching. He preached the first sermon in Ohio that was preached by a representative of the Presbyterian Church. He was the first minister ordained on the north side of the Ohio. He preached the first sermon at t
1 CONC he first meeting of the first Presbytery that met in Ohio, it being his own ordination sermon. He received the first call and was installed the first pastor on the north side of the Ohio. Moreover, he preached the first sermon at 
1 CONC the first meeting of the Presbytery of Cincinnati, and of the Synod of Cincinnati in 1829, and was elected the first moderator of that Presbytery and Synod."
1 CONT
1 CONT Kemper, Willis Miller and Harry Linn Wright. Genealogy of the Kemper Family in the United States. Chicago: Geo K. Hazlitt & Co., 1899.
