0 @N6000000002906217222@ NOTE Willis Miller Kemper, Genealogy of the Fishback Family in America,  (New York: T. M. Taylor, 1914), 114-118, picture appears opposite of page 114.  "The autobiography left by Rev. James Fishback has never been published in full. T
1 CONC he original work, in his own handwriting, is now in the possession of his grandnephew, Mr. Frederick L. Fishback, of Washington, D. C. The latter's father was named James in honor of his uncle, and Frederick L. Fishback, in naming
1 CONC  one of his sons for his father, is also keeping alive the name of the member of the family who, in his day and generation, achieved eminence as a physician, a lawyer and a divine. 
1 CONT      "Dr. Fishback was born in Culpeper County, Virginia, February 4, 1776, the year that the independence of the United States was declared. His parents migrated to Kentucky in 1783 and arrived in October at their temporary home 
1 CONC near the confluence of the Kentucky and Dick's rivers in what is now Garrard County. In 1787 they removed to Fayette County, Kentucky, and made their permanent home about 12 miles east of Lexington. For more than a year, during 17
1 CONC 93-4, young James attended the Transylvania Seminary in Lexington and during 1795-6 his education was continued at a Presbyterian School at Pisgah in Woodford County, which was conducted by Rev. Andrew Steel. Thereafter he chose t
1 CONC he profession of medicine as his calling and entered ardently into the study of the different branches of science appertaining to it. During 1799 and 1800 he attended a course of lectures upon medicine in Philadelphia. Then he ret
1 CONC urned as far as Virginia, where 
1 CONT he practised his profession in Culpeper and Prince William 
1 CONT Counties until October 10, 1801, when he returned to 
1 CONT Kentucky. 
1 CONT      "On May 12, 1802, Dr. Fishback was married to Miss 
1 CONT Dorothea Dandridge Christian, the youngest daughter of 
1 CONT Colonel William and Anne Christian. She was born in Virginia 
1 CONT June 5th, 1785, and her mother was a sister of Patrick 
1 CONT Henry. On June 8, 1841, he married secondly Mrs. Susan H. 
1 CONT Shelby McKinney, the daughter of Gov. Isaac Shelby, of 
1 CONT Kentucky. 
1 CONT Soon after his return to Kentucky, Dr. Fishback established 
1 CONT hi mself in the practice of medicine in Lexington. In 1805 he 
1 CONT was professor of the theory and practice of medicine in the 
1 CONT Transylvania University, but for some reason resigned the 
1 CONT position the following year. In 1808 he was a member of the 
1 CONT State Legislature and by 1810 he says: "The practice of 
1 CONT medicine which I had followed with great assiduity had 
1 CONT become disagreeable to me, and I had for some short time 
1 CONT engaged in the study of law. This I did as much for the knowledge 
1 CONT of the principles of law as for anything else, and I engaged 
1 CONT in the .practice of it up to the end of the year 1816." 
1 CONT We have thus far considered Dr. Fishback as an exponent 
1 CONT of two of the learned professions. In each of these he attained 
1 CONT more than ordinary success and honor. But his career, to 
1 CONT this period of his life, must be regarded as, in a degree, only 
1 CONT preliminary to his major activities in theology. These brought 
1 CONT him even greater distinction. On several occasions in early 
1 CONT life, the pious examples of his parents caused him to consider 
1 CONT seriously the subject of religion, but during his experiences 
1 CONT as a medical student the effects of these influences were 
1 CONT apparently lost. However, he could not have been long established 
1 CONT in Lexington before problems in theology lay firm and 
1 CONT permanent hold upon his mind, for in 1809 we find that he 
1 CONT published an extensive pamphlet by which he 'exploded 
1 CONT Natural Religion and the existence of religion in the world of 
1 CONT Revelation.'
1 CONT      "In 1810 Dr. Fishback united with the Presbyterian church 
1 CONT in Lexington under the pastoral care of Rev. R. H. Cunningham; and very soon organized a Wednesday evening prayer meeting in that church. 
1 CONT      "As it refers to the most vital part of his career it seems best to quote his own account of the controversies that attended Dr. Fishback's entrance upon religious affairs. 
1 CONT      "'In 1813 I published my book entitled 'The philosophy of 
1 CONT the human mind in regard to religion, or a demonstration that 
1 CONT Religion entered the world by Revelation.' 'The book consisted of 306 pages Octavo.' 
1 CONT      "'This volume contained much original matter in defense of the Bible. It excited much attention and great opposition 
1 CONT but was never refuted. It caused me to be summoned before 
1 CONT the Session of the Presbyterian Church in Lexington and 
1 CONT after a long trial I was condemned by the Session for heresy, 
1 CONT from which I took an appeal to the Presbytery and was there 
1 CONT condemned. From that decision I appealed to the Synod 
1 CONT and had the judgment of the lower courts set aside. 
1 CONT      "'Alexander Campbell, who then lived in Brooke County, 
1 CONT Virginia, got one of the books and read it in 1814 or 1815, 
1 CONT and he informed me afterwards that it was the first view he 
1 CONT had ever had of the origin, nature, design and use of the word of God. He at once exploded Natural Religion and adopted the principles fully contained in the Philosophy. It was these principles that he brought to bear so forcefull
1 CONC y and 
1 CONT triumphantly in his debate with R. Owens at Cincinnati 
1 CONT against Atheism. His arguments contain much that is in 
1 CONT my book and in some parts is literally extracted from it. All 
1 CONT that he had said and written that is true in building up his 
1 CONT new Sect he has obtained from the above book. And had he 
1 CONT not have wholly corrupted and perverted the doctrine of 
1 CONT Justification or remission of sin by regarding immersion as 
1 CONT essential to remission he would have done much for the interest of religion. So true is it that a person or church that is wrong on the subject of Justification cannot be right on any other in religion. 
1 CONT      "'Mr. Campbell has raised up a new Sect by dividing the 
1 CONT Baptist Churches and uniting with B. W. Stone who denies 
1 CONT the atonement altogether as consisting in the vicarious sufferings and death of Jesus Christ. The point of their agreement is in the remission of sin in Baptism. 
1 CONT      "'When I joined the Presbyterian Church in 1810 I had not 
1 CONT investigated the subject of Baptism either as to the proper 
1 CONT subjects of it or as to the mode, meaning, or origin of it. 
1 CONT At a subsequent period I attended to it and found that the 
1 CONT Scriptural views of the ordinance had been wholly mistaken 
1 CONT by Pedobaptists, and after examining the Baptist views of 
1 CONT it and the order of the church was convinced that in these 
1 CONT respects the Baptists were right and was baptised at Bryants 
1 CONT the 4th Saturday in November 1816, and on the 4th Saturday 
1 CONT in the next month, December 1816, was licensed to preach the Gospel as a member of the Baptist Church. The month after I joined the first Baptist Church in Lexington, then just 
1 CONT constituted.
1 CONT      "'I now abandoned all other pursuits and gave myself 
1 CONT wholly to the ministry of the word and August 22, 1817, was 
1 CONT ordained in the first Presbyterian Meeting House in Lexington, in which I had seven years before made a profession of religion. I was ordained [FN:  The original certificate of ordination is in the possession of F. L. Fishback, of
1 CONC  Washington, D. C.] by the laying on of hands of a Presbytery composed of Elder Jac. Breath, J. Vardeman and Jas. Welch.'
1 CONT      "The honorary degree of Doctor of Divinity was conferred 
1 CONT upon Dr. Fishback by Transylvania University, July 9, 1823. 
1 CONT From early in May, 1826, until September 27, Dr. Fishback 
1 CONT was absent from Lexington on a tour devoted to the investigations into the methods of religious teachings. He visited Wheeling, Pittsburg, Erie, Buffalo, Schenectady, Albany, Boston, Salem, Andover, Providence, Hartford, New Haven
1 CONC , New York, Philadelphia and Baltimore. 
1 CONT      "Beside giving freely of his time, energy and means to the 
1 CONT upbuilding of the Baptist Church in Lexington, which under his hand rose in ten years from a membership of 8 or 10 to 
1 CONT 175, Dr. Fishback was for 17 years, prior to 1841, pastor of 
1 CONT the Mount Vernon Baptist Church in Woodford County, 
1 CONT Kentucky. 
1 CONT      "Not a little difference of opinion has been expressed as to which of the denominations Dr. Fishback was in doctrinal 
1 CONT accord with at the time of his death. It is safe to say that 
1 CONT the creed or confession of faith of neither the Baptist nor the 
1 CONT Christian (Campbellite) church wholly satisfied one entertaining such positive personal views; however, it is certain that he held essential articles with each. Nevertheless there is no evidence to show that his union with the Bap
1 CONC tist church was ever broken. 
1 CONT      "He died, after a lingering illness, June 26, 1845."
